        UnixSocketTransport.cs
        StdioTransport.cs
        FdTransport.cs
        NamedPipeTransport.cs
    Types/
        EvalResult.cs        \-\- Command result
        VarInfo.cs           \-\- Variable info
        BreakHitEvent.cs     \-\- Debugger break event
        PtyInfo.cs           \-\- PTY session info
        ...
.EE
.SH Architecture
The central class is \f[CR]BashClient\f[R], which implements
\f[CR]IAsyncDisposable\f[R].
It owns an \f[CR]ITransport\f[R] instance and starts a background reader
task at construction time.
.PP
The reader task continuously reads NDJSON lines from the transport and
routes them:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Request/response messages\f[R] complete per\-channel
\f[CR]TaskCompletionSource<JsonElement>\f[R] instances.
When a channel method sends a request, it creates a new
\f[CR]TaskCompletionSource\f[R], registers it, and returns its
\f[CR]Task\f[R].
The reader completes the source when the response arrives.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Server\-push messages\f[R] (observe events, debug break_hit, PTY
output/exit) are dispatched directly to registered event handlers via C#
events.
.PP
Each channel is exposed as a public readonly property:
.IP
.EX
client.Control   \f[I]// ControlChannel  (channel 0)\f[R]
client.Command   \f[I]// CommandChannel  (channel 1)\f[R]
client.State     \f[I]// StateChannel    (channel 2)\f[R]
client.Observe   \f[I]// ObserveChannel  (channel 3)\f[R]
client.Debug     \f[I]// DebugChannel    (channel 4)\f[R]
client.Pty       \f[I]// PtyChannel      (channel 5)\f[R]
.EE
.SH Connection
\f[CR]BashClient\f[R] provides static async factory methods:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// Unix domain socket (default)\f[R]
var client = await BashClient.ConnectAsync(\(dq/tmp/bash\-server\-1000/sock\(dq);

\f[I]// Subprocess (\-\-stdio mode)\f[R]
var client = await BashClient.ConnectStdioAsync(\(dqbash\-server\(dq, \(dq\-\-stdio\(dq);

\f[I]// Inherited file descriptor\f[R]
var client = await BashClient.ConnectFdAsync(3);

\f[I]// Windows Named Pipe (Cygwin)\f[R]
var client = await BashClient.ConnectNamedPipeAsync(\(at\(dq\(rs\(rs.\(rspipe\(rsbash\-server\-myname\(dq);

\f[I]// From existing transport\f[R]
var client = BashClient.FromTransport(myTransport);
.EE
.PP
All async factory methods accept an optional
\f[CR]CancellationToken\f[R] parameter.
\f[CR]FromTransport()\f[R] is synchronous \(en it takes an
already\-connected transport and immediately starts the reader task.
.SH Lifecycle management
C# provides \f[CR]IAsyncDisposable\f[R] with \f[CR]await using\f[R] for
deterministic async cleanup:
.IP
.EX
await \f[B]using\f[R] var client = await BashClient.ConnectAsync(socketPath);
await client.AuthAsync(token);
var result = await client.EvalAsync(\(dqecho hello\(dq);
Console.WriteLine(result.Stdout);
\f[I]// DisposeAsync() called automatically at end of scope\f[R]
.EE
.PP
For environments where \f[CR]await using\f[R] is not available, use
\f[CR]try\f[R]/\f[CR]finally\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
var client = await BashClient.ConnectAsync(socketPath);
\f[B]try\f[R]
{
    await client.AuthAsync(token);
    var result = await client.EvalAsync(\(dqecho hello\(dq);
    Console.WriteLine(result.Stdout);
}
\f[B]finally\f[R]
{
    await client.DisposeAsync();
}
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]DisposeAsync()\f[R] method:
.IP "1." 3
Cancels the reader task via a \f[CR]CancellationTokenSource\f[R].
.IP "2." 3
Sends a \f[CR]disconnect\f[R] message (ignoring errors).
.IP "3." 3
Closes the transport.
.IP "4." 3
Awaits the reader task to ensure clean shutdown.
.PP
\f[CR]DisposeAsync()\f[R] is safe to call multiple times.
.SH Authentication
.IP
.EX
await client.AuthAsync(token);
.EE
.PP
The token is a 64\-character hex string.
Throws \f[CR]AuthException\f[R] on failure.
Check authentication status:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]if\f[R] (client.IsAuthenticated)
{
    \f[I]// safe to use all channels\f[R]
}
.EE
