.SH CONTROL channel (channel 0)
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// Authenticate\f[R]
await client.AuthAsync(token);

\f[I]// Ping\f[R]
await client.PingAsync();

\f[I]// Configure\f[R]
await client.Control.ConfigureAsync(\f[B]new\f[R] { observe_level = 1 });

\f[I]// Disconnect\f[R]
await client.Control.DisconnectAsync();
.EE
.SH COMMAND channel (channel 1)
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// Simple eval (convenience on client)\f[R]
var result = await client.EvalAsync(\(dqecho hello\(dq);
Console.WriteLine(result.Stdout);    \f[I]// \(dqhello\(rsn\(dq\f[R]
Console.WriteLine(result.Stderr);    \f[I]// \(dq\(dq\f[R]
Console.WriteLine(result.ExitCode);  \f[I]// 0\f[R]

\f[I]// With cancellation token\f[R]
\f[B]using\f[R] var cts = \f[B]new\f[R] CancellationTokenSource(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10));
var result2 = await client.EvalAsync(\(dqsleep 2 && echo done\(dq, cts.Token);

\f[I]// Via channel object\f[R]
var result3 = await client.Command.EvalAsync(\(dqls \-la\(dq);
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]EvalAsync()\f[R] method completes when all three response
messages (\f[CR]stdout\f[R], \f[CR]stderr\f[R], \f[CR]complete\f[R])
have been received.
stdout and stderr payloads are automatically base64\-decoded.
.SH STATE channel (channel 2)
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// Variables\f[R]
var info = await client.State.GetVarAsync(\(dqHOME\(dq);
Console.WriteLine(info.Value);        \f[I]// \(dq/home/user\(dq\f[R]
Console.WriteLine(info.Attributes);   \f[I]// []\f[R]

await client.State.SetVarAsync(\(dqMY_VAR\(dq, \(dqhello\(dq, \f[B]new\f[R][] { \(dq\-x\(dq });
await client.State.UnsetVarAsync(\(dqMY_VAR\(dq);

\f[I]// Functions\f[R]
var func = await client.State.GetFuncAsync(\(dqmy_function\(dq);
Console.WriteLine(func.Definition);

await client.State.UnsetFuncAsync(\(dqmy_function\(dq);

\f[I]// Aliases\f[R]
var alias = await client.State.GetAliasAsync(\(dqll\(dq);
await client.State.SetAliasAsync(\(dqll\(dq, \(dqls \-la \-\-color\(dq);
await client.State.UnsetAliasAsync(\(dqll\(dq);

\f[I]// Traps\f[R]
await client.State.SetTrapAsync(\(dqSIGINT\(dq, \(dqecho interrupted\(dq);
await client.State.UnsetTrapAsync(\(dqSIGINT\(dq);

\f[I]// Inspect\f[R]
var items = await client.State.InspectAsync(\(dqvariables\(dq);
.EE
.SH OBSERVE channel (channel 3)
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// Subscribe\f[R]
await client.Observe.SubscribeAsync(1);

\f[I]// Register event handlers (C# events)\f[R]
client.Observe.PreCommand += (sender, e) =>
{
    Console.WriteLine($\(dq[{e.Seq}] running: {e.Command} in {e.Cwd}\(dq);
};

client.Observe.PostCommand += (sender, e) =>
{
    Console.WriteLine($\(dq[{e.Seq}] done: exit={e.ExitStatus} duration={e.DurationMs}ms\(dq);
};

\f[I]// Execute commands \-\- handlers fire automatically\f[R]
await client.EvalAsync(\(dqecho hello\(dq);

\f[I]// Unsubscribe\f[R]
await client.Observe.UnsubscribeAsync();
.EE
.PP
Event handlers follow the standard .NET \f[CR]EventHandler<T>\f[R]
pattern.
They are invoked on the reader task\(cqs thread pool context.
.SH DEBUG channel (channel 4)
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// Enable\f[R]
await client.Debug.EnableAsync();

\f[I]// Status\f[R]
var status = await client.Debug.StatusAsync();
Console.WriteLine($\(dqactive={status.Active} mode={status.Mode}\(dq);

\f[I]// Add breakpoints\f[R]
int bpId = await client.Debug.AddBreakpointAsync(\(dqcommand\(dq, pattern: \(dqecho*\(dq);
int bpId2 = await client.Debug.AddBreakpointAsync(\(dqline\(dq, line: 10);

\f[I]// List breakpoints\f[R]
var bps = await client.Debug.ListBreakpointsAsync();
\f[B]foreach\f[R] (var bp \f[B]in\f[R] bps)
{
    Console.WriteLine($\(dq#{bp.Id} {bp.Kind} hits={bp.HitCount}\(dq);
}

\f[I]// Break hit event\f[R]
client.Debug.BreakHit += (sender, e) =>
{
    Console.WriteLine($\(dqbreak at line {e.Line}: {e.Command}\(dq);
};

\f[I]// Execution control\f[R]
await client.Debug.ContinueAsync();
await client.Debug.StepAsync();
await client.Debug.NextAsync();
await client.Debug.FinishAsync();
await client.Debug.SkipAsync();

\f[I]// Inspect AST\f[R]
var ast = await client.Debug.InspectAstAsync();

\f[I]// Remove / toggle breakpoints\f[R]
await client.Debug.RemoveBreakpointAsync(bpId);
await client.Debug.DisableBreakpointAsync(bpId2);
await client.Debug.EnableBreakpointAsync(bpId2);

\f[I]// Disable debugger\f[R]
await client.Debug.DisableAsync();
.EE
.PP
Note that \f[CR]ContinueAsync()\f[R] is used instead of
\f[CR]continue()\f[R] because \f[CR]continue\f[R] is a reserved keyword
in C#.
.SH PTY channel (channel 5)
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// Spawn\f[R]
var info = await client.Pty.SpawnAsync(\f[B]new\f[R] PtyOpts
{
    Rows = 24,
    Cols = 80,
    StripAnsi = \f[B]true\f[R]
});
Console.WriteLine($\(dqpid={info.Pid}\(dq);

\f[I]// Output events\f[R]
client.Pty.Output += (sender, data) =>
{
    Console.Write(data);
};

client.Pty.Exit += (sender, exitCode) =>
{
    Console.WriteLine($\(dq\(rsnPTY exited: {exitCode}\(dq);
};

\f[I]// Write input\f[R]
await client.Pty.WriteInputAsync(\(dqls \-la\(rsn\(dq);

\f[I]// Resize\f[R]
await client.Pty.ResizeAsync(48, 120);

\f[I]// Signal\f[R]
await client.Pty.SignalAsync(\(dqSIGINT\(dq);

\f[I]// Close\f[R]
await client.Pty.CloseAsync();
.EE
.SH Callback model
The C# binding uses standard .NET events and delegates for server\-push
notifications:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// Observe events\f[R]
client.Observe.PreCommand += handler;    \f[I]// EventHandler<PreCommandEvent>\f[R]
client.Observe.PostCommand += handler;   \f[I]// EventHandler<PostCommandEvent>\f[R]

\f[I]// Debug events\f[R]
client.Debug.BreakHit += handler;        \f[I]// EventHandler<BreakHitEvent>\f[R]

\f[I]// PTY events\f[R]
client.Pty.Output += handler;            \f[I]// EventHandler<string>\f[R]
client.Pty.Exit += handler;              \f[I]// EventHandler<int>\f[R]

\f[I]// Unregister\f[R]
client.Observe.PreCommand \-= handler;
.EE
.PP
Events follow standard .NET conventions:
.IP \(bu 2
Multiple handlers per event are supported.
.IP \(bu 2
Handlers are invoked in registration order.
.IP \(bu 2
Handlers run on a thread pool thread (from the reader task).
.IP \(bu 2
Keep handlers fast and non\-blocking to avoid stalling dispatch.
.IP \(bu 2
For heavy processing, use \f[CR]Task.Run()\f[R] inside the handler.
.SH Timeout handling
The C# binding does not impose a default timeout.
All async operations accept an optional \f[CR]CancellationToken\f[R]
parameter.
Use \f[CR]CancellationTokenSource\f[R] with a timeout for
deadline\-based control:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// 10\-second timeout for eval\f[R]
\f[B]using\f[R] var cts = \f[B]new\f[R] CancellationTokenSource(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10));
var result = await client.EvalAsync(\(dqslow\-command\(dq, cts.Token);

\f[I]// No timeout (completes when response arrives or client is disposed)\f[R]
var result2 = await client.EvalAsync(\(dqecho hello\(dq);
.EE
.PP
When the token is cancelled, the pending \f[CR]TaskCompletionSource\f[R]
is completed with \f[CR]OperationCanceledException\f[R], which the
channel method wraps in \f[CR]TimeoutException\f[R] if the token expired
due to a timeout.
.SH Concurrency safety
The \f[CR]BashClient\f[R] is safe for concurrent use from multiple
\f[CR]Task\f[R] contexts with the following caveats:
.IP \(bu 2
Different channels can be used concurrently (each has its own response
queue).
.IP \(bu 2
Two tasks awaiting the same channel concurrently may receive each
other\(cqs responses \(en avoid this pattern.
.IP \(bu 2
Event handlers run on a thread pool thread, so they must be thread\-safe
if accessing shared state.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]DisposeAsync()\f[R] is safe to call from any context and will
cancel all pending operations.
