.SH Complete example
.IP
.EX
\f[B]using\f[R] BashServer.Client;
\f[B]using\f[R] BashServer.Client.Types;

await \f[B]using\f[R] var client = await BashClient.ConnectAsync(
    \(dq/tmp/bash\-server\-1000/sock\(dq);

\f[I]// Authenticate\f[R]
await client.AuthAsync(\(dqabcdef0123456789...\(dq);

\f[I]// Simple command\f[R]
var result = await client.EvalAsync(\(dqecho hello world\(dq);
Console.Write(result.Stdout);

\f[I]// Set and read a variable\f[R]
await client.State.SetVarAsync(\(dqGREETING\(dq, \(dqhello\(dq);
var info = await client.State.GetVarAsync(\(dqGREETING\(dq);
Console.WriteLine(info.Value);  \f[I]// \(dqhello\(dq\f[R]

\f[I]// Observe\f[R]
var events = \f[B]new\f[R] List<PostCommandEvent>();
client.Observe.PostCommand += (s, e) => events.Add(e);
await client.Observe.SubscribeAsync(1);
await client.EvalAsync(\(dqtrue\(dq);

\f[I]// Brief pause for push events\f[R]
await Task.Delay(100);
Console.WriteLine($\(dqCaptured {events.Count} events\(dq);
.EE
.SH Comparison with other bindings
The C# binding is most similar to the TypeScript binding in its async
model (both use a promise/task\-based approach with a background
reader), but uses .NET events instead of an EventEmitter:
